<div class="bgEmployibility1Top">
</div>

<div class="hometext bgEmployibility1">


    <div class="">
        <ul class="ulBreadcrumb" style="padding-top:10px;">
            <li><a href="/">Home <span class="fa fa-angle-right"></span></a></li>
            <li><a href="/home/employability_index">Employability Index<span class="fa fa-angle-right"></span></a></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="paraCommon paraHeader"><b>Why This Matters – Why Employability Feels Broken Today</b> </p>
        <p class="paraCommon">Employability didn’t become broken overnight. </p>
        <p class="paraCommon">
            It slowly drifted out of sync with how work, technology, and organizations actually evolved.
            <ul>

                <li>Academic scores worked <b>when careers were linear</b></li>
                <li>Indices multiplied <b>when complexity increased</b></li>
                <li>Employers optimized hiring tools</li>
                <li>Candidates optimized test-taking</li>
                <li>Somewhere in between, <i>meaning</i> was lost</li>
            </ul>

        </p>
        <p class="paraCommon">What once worked well - <b>academic scores, standardized assessments,</b> and <b>early-career filtering</b> - now struggles to explain real-world <b>performance, adaptability,</b> and <b>long-term fit.</b></p>
        <p class="paraCommon"><b>AI makes it possible to move beyond outdated scores and indices by understanding employability as a living, evolving capability rather than a static measurement.</b>  </p>
        <p class="paraCommon">This unpacks <i>how we got here</i> - without blaming individuals on either side.</p>
        <p class="paraCommon"><b><i>If the problem is structural, the solution can’t be another patchwork fix howsoever urgent that may be!</i></b>  </p>
        <p class="paraCommon">  </p>

    </div>
    <p class="paraCommon"> </p>
</div>
